-- machine-in-hyperactive (2026-11-21): Avik: "yes we definitely want it written in hyperactive. .the
-- only question is how to get there". The machine is the paper's §4.5 as definitions of the language:
-- the rules a function of the two cells that met and nothing else, the memory (what is at a wire)
-- separate from the rules, and the executor (which wire steps next, and which wire names a step may
-- use) separate from both, so that an executor for any hardware is a different choice over the same
-- rules. Known (2026-11-21): the smallest such machine is written, module kernel under lib, names
-- nk-*, loaded after the library. A wire is a slot: empty, a value, an operation, or the pair that
-- met there. Values are a closure (a body and the wires of its environment, as the old machine's λ
-- cell), a number, a reference to a held wire; operations are apply, increment, duplicate, erase,
-- forward. nk-rule is every rule; nk-plan is a step at one wire (the rule, or a read of a held wire;
-- a read of a wire that holds nothing yet asks nothing: waiting is not a step); nk-do is the memory,
-- each request changing one wire by what was there; NkExec is the one-core executor. It runs in the
-- language: inc ((λg. (λf. f (f x)) (λy. g y)) (λz. inc z)) at 5 gives 8, f a closure with a part,
-- held once and read twice; a term whose size is a run-time input gives 107 for 6 applications at
-- 100. Proven: the memory's two laws (nk-get-set: a wire just written reads as what was written;
-- nk-get-set-other: writing one wire leaves every other wire's reading unchanged). The module checks
-- whole: 2888 passed, 0 failed (`hyperactive check` on it). Emitted through the C experiment, the
-- machine running a fixed term comes out as one C function, v0 + 3: the specializer runs the machine
-- away at build time, so the machine with the specializer compiles a net term to C. With the term a
-- run-time input the emission did not finish; measured, not guessed: the specializer alone ends in
-- under a minute and its result weighs 1,496,845 nodes (nk-spec, c-weight), and the machine's own
-- counters (CUBICAL_PROFILE=show, printed on SIGTERM) put the rest in the collection of a code's
-- globals (cg-acc, cg-list), the evaluator as a term (eval-g, quote) and the lift pass (x-frees-at,
-- x-lift-n), memory growing about 100 MB a second: the default schedule unfolds the machine's
-- definitions into one term, and the stages after it are run over that. To do there: a schedule
-- that leaves the machine's definitions as calls. Not in the machine: neutrals, dimensions, systems,
-- composition, data, the freeing of held wires. Proven too (nk-do-swap, with nk-upd-swap, nk-do-same,
-- nk-do-upd under it): two plans that write different wires, done in either order, leave memories
-- that read the same at every wire; it uses the two laws and nothing about which rules made the
-- plans. nk-step-swap: steps at two wires commute, given that the plans write different wires and
-- that each plan is the same whether or not the other step went first. The module then checks
-- 2903 passed, 0 failed. nk-step-commute: the same from apartness alone, no hypothesis about plans
-- left: if the two plans write different wires, and each writes neither the other step's wire nor
-- the wire the other reads (nk-rw: the held wire, for apply or increment facing a reference),
-- the steps commute. Under it nk-get-do-apart (a wire a plan writes nowhere reads the same after
-- it), nk-plan-at (a plan reads the memory at that one wire only), nk-plan-stable. The module
-- checks 2910 passed, 0 failed. Not proven: that the wire names an executor hands out and the
-- linearity of the net (each wire held by one writer and one reader) keep plans
-- apart; that a step preserves the meaning of
-- the net, which needs the meaning of a net as a term first. Learned about the tools: the checker
-- refuses, with no message, a recursion that is not on a constructor; the source finds every name
-- across all modules, constructors included, so a new name is checked with `hyperactive history
-- NAME` before it is written; a refused write prints names only, and `hyperactive check` on the
-- module, or `view ... type T TERM` on the failing term, prints the reason.
-- Next, in order, each with how (2026-11-21, written for the session that continues this):
-- (a) Done 2026-11-21: nk-step-commute. What remains of commuting is the apartness itself: state
-- linearity of a memory (every wire named by at most one value-writer and one operation), prove
-- each rule keeps it given fresh names, and that it gives nk-aparts for two different pairs.
-- (b) The meaning of a net as a term. Read back by wire: a wire holding a value denotes that value
-- (a closure denotes its body under the denotations of its environment's wires; a reference denotes
-- what the held wire denotes); an apply waiting at f with argument a makes its out wire denote f
-- applied to a; a duplicate at w makes both its wires denote what w denotes; increment and forward
-- likewise; a pair at a wire reads as its operation waiting on its value. State it as an inductive
-- relation between a memory, a wire and a term, then prove for apply meeting a closure that every
-- wire denotes after the step what it denoted before, up to one β step; the lemma underneath is
-- that nk-build of a body in an environment makes out denote the body under that environment.
-- (c) The machine itself as C. Read specialize and sched-default (unread) for how a schedule keeps a
-- definition as a call; nk-spec measures the specializer alone; then the emitter needs a template
-- for the word eliminator (cwelim: a loop from 0 to w carrying the accumulator) because toNat is
-- built from it, or the step count is passed as constructors. Then closures, memory, the world's
-- commands in the emitter (hyperactive-emits-c).
-- (d) Grow the machine toward the paper's §4.5 in this order: neutrals (a stuck operation becomes a
-- value holding its operands), pairs and projections, data and case, then dimensions and systems;
-- each rule added with its proof, nk-do-swap already covering every one of them.
-- (e) Beside it, not started: the checker's soundness (the ptr-of hole) and the store by content;
-- see language-implementation-remainder. The notes page outside the repository, for Avik's reading
-- only, is https://claude.ai/artifact/GsZCMdHv512PpU6qERyCmD; nothing on it is absent from here.
machine-in-hyperactive : Node
machine-in-hyperactive = node "The machine is written in hyperactive: its rules, its memory and its executor separate definitions, each rule proven to preserve the meaning of the net and steps at different wires proven to commute." (cons language-implementation-remainder nil)
